<h2>Who buys organic cow manure from India, and why?</h2>
<p>Two buyer types make up almost all of this trade. Horticulture and garden-retail businesses in Europe and North America want a natural soil-improvement input, usually in retail-ready bags. Plantation agriculture operations in the Gulf, Maldives and Southeast Asia want bulk organic matter to build up soil that's naturally low in organic carbon. If you're reading this as either type of buyer, the sourcing conversation is largely the same — the packing conversation is not.</p>

<h2>What does "organic" actually mean here?</h2>
<p>Worth being direct about this upfront, because it's the most common point of confusion. When we use "organic manure" or "organic fertilizer," we're describing the product type — animal-origin, non-synthetic. We are not claiming certification under NPOP, USDA Organic, EU Organic or any comparable scheme, and we don't hold such certification. If your buyer or your market specifically requires certified product, raise it before you order — we'd rather tell you honestly at the enquiry stage than have a shipment rejected at your border.</p>

<h2>Which form should you order?</h2>
<p>Composted, sun-dried cow manure ships as powder, granules or cakes/pellets. Powder and granules dominate export volume because they're easier to handle, spread with standard equipment, and store without degrading. Cakes are a smaller, more traditional-use request. The form you choose affects both the container loading calculation and the price — finer processing costs more.</p>

<h2>The import permit question comes before everything else</h2>
<p>This is worth raising before you get into specification or pricing conversations, because it can be a hard stop if you skip it. Animal-origin fertilizers face significantly tighter import controls in most countries than plant-origin agricultural products — many require a prior import permit, documented composting or heat-treatment declarations, or additional wording on the phytosanitary certificate. Some markets are straightforward. Others are effectively closed to this product category without the right paperwork arranged well in advance.</p>
<p>Check with your national plant-protection or agriculture authority before placing an order, and share what you find with us — we'll confirm what documentation we can provide to match.</p>

<h2>Specification checklist</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Form</strong> — powder, granule or cake</li>
  <li><strong>Moisture maximum</strong> — 15-25% is typical; lower moisture means more usable material per shipped tonne</li>
  <li><strong>Screening mesh</strong> — 2mm, 4mm or your own requirement, to remove stones and debris</li>
  <li><strong>NPK range</strong> — natural-material variation, confirmable via certificate of analysis per lot</li>
  <li><strong>Packing</strong> — bulk jumbo bags for agricultural use, or retail bags with your artwork for garden-centre programmes</li>
</ul>

<h2>Where does India ship organic manure?</h2>
<p>Verified trade data for HS 3101 shows Italy, France and the United States among reported destination markets, with the Maldives, Singapore and several Gulf countries mentioned in secondary sources for plantation use, though without individually confirmed figures. We're being direct that this particular dataset is lower-confidence than some of our others — it comes from trade-data aggregator compilations rather than a direct government source query, and we've flagged that in the underlying data rather than presenting the numbers as more certain than they are.</p>
